/-!
# The order of the McLaughlin permutation model

The exact ATLAS model `TauCeti.Sporadic.McL` has order `898128000` (`TauCeti.Sporadic.card_McL`).
Along the points `0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5` its basic orbits have lengths `275, 112, 81, 36, 5, 2` and the
final pointwise stabilizer is trivial. `McLaughlin.Orbits` establishes all of these but the fifth,
which this file supplies: the orbit of `4` under the pointwise stabilizer of `{0, 1, 2, 3}` is
`{4, 37, 40, 102, 119}`, so that this stabilizer has order `10`. Through the surjection
`exists_surjective_McL`, the presented McLaughlin group has at least `898128000` elements whenever
it is finite (`TauCeti.Sporadic.le_natCard_group_McL`).

The orbit contains the five points because an explicit word of order five fixes `0, 1, 2, 3` and
cycles them. That it contains no other point cannot be read off the McLaughlin graph, whose
automorphism group `McL:2` is twice as large; it needs an invariant of `McL` that the outer
automorphism does not preserve. The invariant used is a family of `2025` sets of `22` points,
listed as the images of one seed set under words in the generators, and a kernel computation
checks that each generator permutes the family, so that every element of `McL` does. (These sets
are one of the two `McL`-orbits of `22`-point cocliques of the graph, the fixed sets of one class
of subgroups `M₂₂`; the proof does not use this.) For `p < 4`, the number of points of a set
adjacent to `p` does not change when an element of `McL` fixing `p` moves the set. Every point
other than `0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 37, 40, 102, 119` lies on a set of the family whose four adjacency
counts occur on no set through `4`, so no element of `McL` fixing `0, 1, 2, 3` sends `4` there.

Adjacency is the relation `related` of `McLaughlin.Orbits`, whose invariance under `McL` is
proved there. The words, tables and witnesses were found by computer search; only their
kernel-checked evaluations enter the proofs.

## References

* R. A. Wilson, R. A. Parker, J. N. Bray et al., *ATLAS of Finite Group Representations*,
  version 3, representation `McLG1-p275B0` and the maximal subgroups of `McL`,
  <https://brauer.maths.qmul.ac.uk/Atlas/v3/spor/McL/>.
* Á. Seress, *Permutation Group Algorithms*, Cambridge Tracts in Mathematics 152,
  Cambridge University Press, 2003, §4.1.
-/

